Input

Flags:
  -i, --input FORMAT            Required. One of jsonl, csv or tsv.
      --map EXPR                Run this jq expression on each record and send its
                                result as the state. Map only the text a person would
                                read.
      --id EXPR                 Name each record with the string or number this jq
                                expression returns.
      --delimiter CHAR          Set the field separator for -i csv: auto, or one
                                character. Defaults to a comma.
      --image NAME=PATH         Put a PNG or JPEG in every record's state under NAME.
                                Repeatable. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --image-field NAME        Read a PNG or JPEG per record from the path this state
                                key holds. Repeatable. Experimental on TypeSafe's side.
      --skip-blank              Drop blank lines.

How they work together:
  Calibration requires JSONL, CSV or TSV records on stdin. --map runs a jq expression on
  each original record and sends its result as the state. Map only the text a person
  would read. Object keys come out sorted. The result may nest max-map-depth levels deep
  and encode to max-line-bytes, as version lists. Labels are selected from the original
  record.

  --id runs one record at a time as input is read. Its result must be a string or
  number. Keep it cheap, such as a field lookup. version lists max-id-bytes.
  --skip-blank drops blank lines without emitting a result. The default field separator
  is ','. --delimiter auto detects the separator of CSV input. An explicit delimiter
  takes one character. TSV uses tabs.

  --image NAME=PATH adds a PNG or JPEG under NAME. Streams read stdin and add the fixed
  image to every record. --image-field reads a PNG or JPEG path from a state key per
  record. Image support is experimental on the TypeSafe side. See version for the image
  and file limits.

